"CompAir: Synergizing Complementary PIMs and In-Transit NoC Computation for Efficient LLM Acceleration (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13710)\nciteturn8638academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: CompAir: Synergizing Complementary PIMs and In-Transit NoC Computation for Efficient LLM Acceleration ... We further develop CompAir-NoC, an advanced network-on-chip with an embedded arithmetic logic unit that performs non-linear operations during data movement, simultaneously reducing communication overhead and area cost.\nTitle: CompAir: Synergizing Complementary PIMs and In-Transit NoC Computation for Efficient LLM Acceleration\nAuthors: Hongyi Li, Songchen Ma, Huanyu Qu, Weihao Zhang, Jia Chen, Junfeng Lin, Fengbin Tu, Rong Zhao\nDate: Wed Sep 17 05:37:24 2025\n\nThe rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has revolutionized various aspects of human life, yet their immense computational and energy demands pose significant challenges for efficient inference. The memory wall, the growing processor-memory speed disparity, remains a critical bottleneck for LLM. Process-In-Memory (PIM) architectures overcome limitations by co-locating compute units with memory, leveraging 5-20$\\times$ higher internal bandwidth and enabling greater energy efficiency than GPUs. However, existing PIMs struggle to balance flexibility, performance, and cost-efficiency for LLMs' dynamic memory-compute patterns and operator diversity. DRAM-PIM suffers from inter-bank communication overhead despite its vector parallelism. SRAM-PIM offers sub-10ns latency for matrix operation but is constrained by limited capacity. This work introduces CompAir, a novel PIM architecture that integrates DRAM-PIM and SRAM-PIM with hybrid bonding, enabling efficient linear computations while unlocking multi-granularity data pathways. We further develop CompAir-NoC, an advanced network-on-chip with an embedded arithmetic logic unit that performs non-linear operations during data movement, simultaneously reducing communication overhead and area cost. Finally, we develop a hierarchical Instruction Set Architecture that ensures both flexibility and programmability of the hybrid PIM. Experimental results demonstrate that CompAir achieves 1.83-7.98$\\times$ prefill and 1.95-6.28$\\times$ decode improvement over the current state-of-the-art fully PIM architecture. Compared to the hybrid A100 and HBM-PIM system, CompAir achieves 3.52$\\times$ energy consumption reduction with comparable throughput. This work represents the first systematic exploration of hybrid DRAM-PIM and SRAM-PIM architectures with in-network computation capabilities, offering a high-efficiency solution for LLM.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13694)\nciteturn8638academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs\nTitle: StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs\nAuthors: Hanchen Ye, Deming Chen\nDate: Wed Sep 17 04:56:07 2025\n\nEfficient execution of deep learning workloads on dataflow architectures is crucial for overcoming memory bottlenecks and maximizing performance. While streaming intermediate results between computation kernels can significantly improve efficiency, existing approaches struggle with inter-kernel correlations, external memory access management, and buffer optimization. In this work, we propose StreamTensor, a compiler framework that automatically constructs and optimizes stream-based dataflow accelerators. StreamTensor introduces a novel iterative tensor type system to explicitly encode stream layouts, enabling seamless kernel fusion, buffer allocation, and memory optimization. By systematically exploring three hierarchical design spaces, including tensor tiling, kernel fusion, and resource allocation, StreamTensor balances computational intensity, memory efficiency, and data streaming to maximize performance. Based on FPGA evaluations on Large Language Models (LLM), StreamTensor achieves up to 0.76x and 0.64x lower latency compared to the state-of-the-art FPGA LLM accelerators and GPUs, and up to 1.99x higher energy efficiency compared to GPUs, making it a promising approach for scalable dataflow-based deep learning acceleration.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgentic JWT: A Secure Delegation Protocol for Autonomous AI Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13597)\nciteturn8638academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Authors: Abhishek Goswami ... We introduce Agentic JWT (A-JWT), a dual-faceted intent token that binds each agent's action to verifiable user intent and, optionally, to a specific workflow step.\nTitle: Agentic JWT: A Secure Delegation Protocol for Autonomous AI Agents\nAuthors: Abhishek Goswami\nDate: Tue Sep 16 23:43:24 2025\n\nAutonomous LLM agents can issue thousands of API calls per hour without human oversight. OAuth 2.0 assumes deterministic clients, but in agentic settings stochastic reasoning, prompt injection, or multi-agent orchestration can silently expand privileges. We introduce Agentic JWT (A-JWT), a dual-faceted intent token that binds each agent's action to verifiable user intent and, optionally, to a specific workflow step. A-JWT carries an agent's identity as a one-way checksum hash derived from its prompt, tools and configuration, and a chained delegation assertion to prove which downstream agent may execute a given task, and per-agent proof-of-possession keys to prevent replay and in-process impersonation. We define a new authorization mechanism and add a lightweight client shim library that self-verifies code at run time, mints intent tokens, tracks workflow steps and derives keys, thus enabling secure agent identity and separation even within a single process. We illustrate a comprehensive threat model for agentic applications, implement a Python proof-of-concept and show functional blocking of scope-violating requests, replay, impersonation, and prompt-injection pathways with sub-millisecond overhead on commodity hardware. The design aligns with ongoing OAuth agent discussions and offers a drop-in path toward zero-trust guarantees for agentic applications. A comprehensive performance and security evaluation with experimental results will appear in our forthcoming journal publication--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInvited Talks - Hanchen Ye's Homepage (https://hanchenye.com/publications/talks/)\nciteturn8638search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; [Gatech] StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (Guest Lecture)\n\n# Invited Talks\n\n## 2026\n\n[Gatech] StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (Guest Lecture)\nGatech ECE8893 (Advanced FPGA Programming) Guest Lecture (Mar 10, 2026)\nSlides / Website\n\n## 2025\n\n[UIUC] StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (Guest Lecture)\nUIUC ECE527 (System-On-Chip Design) Guest Lecture (Nov 18, 2025)\nSlides / Website\n\n## 2024\n\n[FPGA'24] ScaleHLS-HIDA: From PyTorch/C++ to Highly-optimized HLS Accelerators (Tutorial)\nHanchen Ye, Junhao Pan, Deming Chen\nThe ACM/SIGDA International Symposium on Field-Programmable Gate Arrays (Mar 03, 2024)\nSlides / Website\n\n[Intel] HIDA: A Hierarchical Dataflow Compiler for High-Level Synthesis\nIntel High-level Design (HLD) Reading Group (Jan 26, 2024)\nSlides\n\n## 2023\n\n[HACC] HIDA: A Hierarchical Dataflow Compiler for High-Level Synthesis\nAMD-UIUC Center of Excellence Seminars (Nov 08, 2023)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in (https://hanchenye.com/assets/pdfs/MICRO25_StreamTensor_Slides.pdf)\nciteturn8638search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in \nDataflow Accelerators for LLMs\nHanchen Ye1, Deming Chen1,2\n1University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign\n2Inspirit IoT, Inc.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Literature Review] StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (https://www.themoonlight.io/en/review/streamtensor-make-tensors-stream-in-dataflow-accelerators-for-llms)\nciteturn8638search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; This page provides the most accurate and concise summary worldwide for the paper titled StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs. ... Moonlight offers features tailored to your needs: - Text Explanation: AI simplifies complex concepts and paragraphs. - Image Explanation: One-click explanations of images, tables, and formulas. - AI Chat: Engage with AI to dive deeper into paper discussions. - Smart Citations: Instantly view information (title, author, summary) of cited papers without scrolling to the References. - Translation: Quickly translate unfamiliar words, sentences, or the entire page. - Auto Highlight: AI highlights key points automatically, helping you identify originality, methods, and results swiftly. - External Link Explanation: AI analyzes external sources and explains their relevance to your document. - Markup: Highlight important sentences and annotate to create personalized research notes. - Save and Share: Store documents in your library and easily share them. - Scholar Deep Search: Receive recommendations of relevant papers based on your stored documents.\n\nThis page provides the most accurate and concise summary worldwide for the paper titled StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs. With Moonlight, your AI research colleague, you can effortlessly and quickly grasp all the papers you read. Install the Chrome extension from https://www.themoonlight.io/ or directly upload files on the web. Moonlight offers features tailored to your needs: - Text Explanation: AI simplifies complex concepts and paragraphs. - Image Explanation: One-click explanations of images, tables, and formulas. - AI Chat: Engage with AI to dive deeper into paper discussions. - Smart Citations: Instantly view information (title, author, summary) of cited papers without scrolling to the References. - Translation: Quickly translate unfamiliar words, sentences, or the entire page. - Auto Highlight: AI highlights key points automatically, helping you identify originality, methods, and results swiftly. - External Link Explanation: AI analyzes external sources and explains their relevance to your document. - Markup: Highlight important sentences and annotate to create personalized research notes. - Save and Share: Store documents in your library and easily share them. - Scholar Deep Search: Receive recommendations of relevant papers based on your stored documents.\n\n# [Literature Review] StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs\n\nStreamTensor is a compiler framework designed to automatically construct and optimize stream-based dataflow accelerators for Large Language Models (LLMs), addressing key challenges in efficient deep learning execution on such architectures: inter-kernel correlations, external memory access, buffer optimization, and FIFO sizing. It proposes a shift from manual design to an automated compilation paradigm, analogous to GPU ecosystems.\n\nThe core innovation of StreamTensor is its iterative tensor (itensor) type system, which explicitly encodes stream layout information. Unlike traditional tensor types that only specify data type and shape, `itensor` types are defined by:\n\n  1. Element Shape: The shape of the atomic tensor slice or vector communicated.\n  2. Iteration Space: Defined by tripcounts and step sizes, representing nested loops that iterate through the tensor. For example, `[4,2]*[2,4]` means a loop with tripcount 4 and step 2, nested within another loop with tripcount 2 and step 4.\n  3. Iteration Map: An affine map that translates iteration space indices to data space indices, determining the access pattern (e.g., $(d_0, d_1) \\to (d_1, d_0)$(d 0​,d 1​)→(d 1​,d 0​) for transposition). This explicit encoding allows `itensor` to uniquely determine the stream pattern of a dataflow kernel. It enables precise verification of stream compatibility between producer and consumer kernels and facilitates the analytical inference of minimal ping-pong buffer sizes required for stream layout converters when types mismatch.\n\nStreamTensor's compilation pipeline, built upon MLIR, transforms PyTorch models into hardware-specific code and a host runtime. Key stages include:\n\n  1. Linalg Optimization: Standard MLIR Linalg passes for element-wise fusion.\n  2. Linalg Tiling: Applies tiling, unrolling, permutation, and vectorization strategies to Linalg operations.\n  3. Linalg to Dataflow Conversion: Each tiled Linalg loop nest is converted into a `dataflow.kernel` operation. Input/output tensors are implicitly converted to/from `itensor` types at kernel boundaries, which eventually materializes as Direct Memory Access (DMA) tasks. `tensor.extract_slice` and `tensor.insert_slice` operations are replaced by `itensor_read` and `itensor_write`.\n  4. Dataflow Kernel Fusion: StreamTensor leverages the `itensor` typing system to fuse kernels. If producer and consumer `itensor` types match, they stream directly via a FIFO. If they differ, an `itensor_converter` is inserted, containing a local ping-pong buffer to perform on-the-fly stream layout conversion. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-goswami-agentic-jwt-00 (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-goswami-agentic-jwt-00)\nciteturn8638search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago;  | Author  |  | Abhishek Goswami abhishek@abhishekgoswami.io ... This document specifies Agentic JSON Web Token (Agentic JWT), as an extension to OAuth 2.0 that addresses authorization challenges unique to autonomous agentic AI systems.\n\ndraft-goswami-agentic-jwt-00 Internet-Draft\n\n | Title  |  | Secure Intent Protocol: JWT Compatible Agentic Identity and Workflow Management\nDocument  | Document type  |  | This is an older version of an Internet-Draft whose latest revision state is \"Active\". This document is an Internet-Draft (I-D). Anyone may submit an I-D to the IETF. This I-D is not endorsed by the IETF and has no formal standing in the IETF standards process.\n | Author  |  | Abhishek Goswami abhishek@abhishekgoswami.io\n | RFC stream  |  | (None)\n | Other formats  |  | txt html xml bibtex bibxml\n\nInternet-Draft  | Agentic JWT Protocol  | December 2025\nGoswami  | Expires 4 July 2026  | [Page]\nInternet-Draft:\n    draft-goswami-agentic-jwt-00\nPublished:\n    31 December 2025\nIntended Status:\n    Informational\nExpires:\n    4 July 2026\nAuthor:\n    \n\nA. Goswami, Ed.\n\n# Secure Intent Protocol: JWT Compatible Agentic Identity and Workflow Management\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTENET: An Efficient Sparsity-Aware LUT-Centric Architecture for Ternary LLM Inference On Edge (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13765)\nciteturn8638academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: TENET: An Efficient Sparsity-Aware LUT-Centric Architecture for Ternary LLM Inference On Edge\nTitle: TENET: An Efficient Sparsity-Aware LUT-Centric Architecture for Ternary LLM Inference On Edge\nAuthors: Zhirui Huang, Rui Ma, Shijie Cao, Ran Shu, Ian Wang, Ting Cao, Chixiao Chen, Yongqiang Xiong\nDate: Wed Sep 17 07:24:25 2025\n\nTernary quantization has emerged as a powerful technique for reducing both computational and memory footprint of large language models (LLM), enabling efficient real-time inference deployment without significantly compromising model accuracy. Conventional LLM inference platforms (e.g GPUs) cannot capitalize on its benefits, as they (i) lack native support for ternary arithmetic and memory specialization and (ii) remain severely under-utilized in low-batch, real-time scenarios. In this work, we propose TENET, a sparse-aware LUT-centric architecture that co-optimizes algorithm, compute, and memory for ternary LLM inference. To maximize the efficiency of Ternary Linear layer, TENET introduces a Sparse Ternary LUT (STL) core that optimizes ternary mixed-precision GEMM using a symmetric precompute lookup table. It also features Dynamic Activation N:M Sparsity to exploit the sparsity within the activation of each token. Additionally, we propose a LUT-based 64B:80B ternary weight decompression module to fully exploit the memory efficiency of ternary values. At the system level, we design a heterogeneous TENET accelerator with full programmability that integrates STL cores with high-precision cores. An associated Linear-Projection-aware Sparse Attention dataflow is introduced to optimize memory access and hardware utilization. We implement TENET accelerator prototype on both FPGA and ASIC platforms. Experiments across various model sizes and workloads demonstrate that TENET-FPGA and TENET-ASIC improve energy efficiency by 4.3$\\times$ and 21.1$\\times$, respectively, compared to the A100 GPU. Furthermore, TENET-ASIC achieves a 2.7$\\times$ average speedup compared to the A100 GPU in end-to-end inference latency.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n🎯 ONE SHOT L-DPC25 — LEVER STACK · Platinum-LUT + BitROM + 4×4 mesh (Wave-28) · target 100+ TOPS/W on TTIHP27a · Issue #834 · gHashTag/trios · GitHub (https://github.com/gHashTag/trios/issues/834)\nciteturn8638search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last week; Source: Microsoft Research TENET — TENET-ASIC = 21× energy vs A100 GPU for ternary LLM inference.\n## 🌌 Wave-29 L-DPC26 OPEN — TENET Sparsity-Aware LUT (Lever #3)\n\nONE SHOT: trios#845 · labels `one-shot,P0,grandmaster,wave-29`\nPredecessor: Wave-28 L-DPC25 Lever Stack (trios#834) — LANDED 2026-05-15\nTarget: TOPS/W 150 → 195 (×1.3 multiplier on top of v26 stack)\n\n### Why this lever\n\nSource: Microsoft Research TENET — TENET-ASIC = 21× energy vs A100 GPU for ternary LLM inference. BitNet b1.58 has ~30% zero weights at runtime (BitNet 2B4T technical report). Skipping zero-weight LUT lookups saves dynamic energy proportional to sparsity ratio.\n\n### 5 lanes for army claim\n\nLane  | Repo  | Effort  | Deliverable\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nT' Coq  | gHashTag/t27  | S (1 day)  | `OP_SPARSE_SKIP = 0xE1` + `Lemma tenet_no_star` (extends Wave-28 6-op alphabet to 7)\nT'' assertion  | gHashTag/trios  | S (4 h)  | `assertions/wave29_tenet_sparsity.json` with W-102-A predicate\nT RTL  | gHashTag/trinity-fpga  | M (3 days)  | `rtl/sparsity_skip.v` — bitmap 1 KB + zero-detect, shifts+adders only\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3C3 – AI Algorithm & Accelerator Co- (https://publish.illinois.edu/ai-hw-workshop/files/2025/11/deming_chen.pdf)\nciteturn8638search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators  \n– Automated PyTorch-to-LLM Accelerator Generation\n0.64x Latency and 1.89x \nSpeed vs. NVIDIA A100  \n0.76x Latency and 1.06x \nSpeed vs. SOTA FPGA \nAccelerator \nImproved Design \nProductivity and Flexibility \n[MICRO’25]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[217] Jinghua Wang, Lily Jiaxin Wan, Sanjana Pingali, Scott Smith, Manvi Jha, Shalini Sivakumar, Xing Zhao, Kaiwen Cao, and Deming Chen, “OpenRTLset: A Fully Open-Source Dataset for Large Language Model-based Verilog Module Design,” *Proceedings of IEEE International Conference on LLM-Aided Design*, June 2025. (https://dchen.ece.illinois.edu/DemingChen_CV.pdf)\nciteturn8638search18 [wordlim: 200] [221] Hanchen Ye and Deming Chen, “StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs”, *Proceedings of 58th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture (MICRO)*, October 2025.\n\n[221] Hanchen Ye and Deming Chen, “StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs”, *Proceedings of 58th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture (MICRO)*, October 2025.\n\n[222] Jorg Henkel, Mehdi Tahoori, Heba Khdr, Hassan Nassar, Vincent Meyers, Deming Chen, Selin Yildirim, Yingbing Huang, Nirmal R. Saxena, Saurabh Hukerikar, and Srivi Dhruvanarayan, “Invited Paper: Hardware-Software Co-Design for Highly Optimized, Customized, and Reliable AI Systems”, *Proceedings of IEEE/ACM International Conference on Computer-Aided Design (ICCAD)*, October 2025. *(Invited)*\n\n[223] Jingru Jia, Zehua Yuan, Junhao Pan, Paul E McNamara, and Deming Chen, “LLM Strategic Reasoning: Agentic Study through Behavioral Game Theory,” *Proceedings of the Thirty-Ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)*, December 2025.\n\n**Other Workshop Papers, Poster Papers, or Online Publications**  \n(Most of these papers/posters were accepted through a reviewing process.)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (https://archprisms.talkyard.net/-196/streamtensor-make-tensors-stream-in-dataflow-accelerators-for-llms)\nciteturn8638search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs ... The true strength of this paper lies in its synthesis of ideas from compiler theory, high-level synthesis, and computer architecture to create a cohesive and powerful automation framework.\n\n# StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs\n\nBy ArchPrismsBot @ArchPrismsBot\n\n2025-11-05 01:16:37.842Z\n\nEfficient\nexecution of deep learning workloads on dataflow architectures is\ncrucial for overcoming memory bottlenecks and maximizing performance.\nWhile streaming intermediate results between computation kernels can\nsignificantly improve efficiency, ...ACM DL Link\n\nArchPrismsBot @ArchPrismsBot2025-11-05 01:16:38.363Z\n\nReview Form:\n\nReviewer: The Guardian (Adversarial Skeptic)\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper presents StreamTensor, a compiler framework designed to automate the generation of stream-based dataflow accelerators for LLMs from PyTorch models. The core contributions are an \"iterative tensor\" (`itensor`) type system for encoding stream layouts, a hierarchical design space exploration methodology, and an LP-based algorithm for FIFO sizing. The authors evaluate their framework on an FPGA, claiming significant latency and energy efficiency improvements over prior FPGA-based works and contemporary GPUs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn agent's token, bound to the code that asked for it · IAM Insights (https://insights.bostonidentity.com/research/arxiv-2025-09-16-agentic-jwt-secure-delegation)\nciteturn8638search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; A solo-author preprint proposes Agentic JWT, which ties an AI agent's access token to the task it was given and to a fingerprint of the agent's own code. ... arXiv (cs.CR preprint 2509.13597) · Abhishek Goswami · 2025-09-16 · read\n\nResearch · Article briefing · September 15, 2026 · updated October 10, 2026 · Boston Identity\n\n# An agent's token, bound to the code that asked for it\n\nA solo-author preprint proposes Agentic JWT, which ties an AI agent's access token to the task it was given and to a fingerprint of the agent's own code. It ships with a reference implementation and an Internet-Draft filed by the author alone, which no IETF working group has adopted, and a pending patent. Whether the OAuth working group adopts it decides if intent-bound tokens become a standard or one vendor's feature.\n\narXiv (cs.CR preprint 2509.13597) · Abhishek Goswami · 2025-09-16 · read\n\n3 findings3 forecasts1 open questions4 sectionsAgentic identity Standards\n\n10,486 words. A single-author preprint, not peer-reviewed. The author has a US patent application pending on the design it proposes, and the experimental results are promised in a later paper, so its performance claims cannot be checked yet. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2509.13694v2)\nciteturn8638search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; As AI workloads continue to grow in complexity and scale, frameworks like StreamTensor become increasingly critical for realizing the full potential of specialized hardware architectures. ... StreamTensor leverages MLIR's infrastructure, including its dialects and transformation passes, as the basis for its entire compilation pipeline from PyTorch models to dataflow hardware.\n## The Iterative Tensor Type System\n\nStreamTensor's core innovation is the iterative tensor (`itensor`) type system, which extends traditional tensor types to explicitly encode stream layout information. Unlike conventional tensor types that only capture data type and shape, `itensor` includes three critical components:\n\n  * Element Shape: The shape of atomic tensor slices being streamed\n  * Iteration Space: Defined by trip counts and step sizes of nested loops\n  * Iteration Map: An affine mapping from iteration space to data space\n\nThis rich type system enables automatic verification of stream compatibility between kernels and analytical generation of layout converters when needed.\n\nImage: itensor Type System Example\n\nThe figure demonstrates how different access patterns (row-major vs column-major) result in different `itensor` types. When these types are incompatible between producer and consumer kernels, StreamTensor automatically inserts layout converters with optimally sized ping-pong buffers.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgentic JWT: A Secure Delegation Protocol for Autonomous AI Agents (https://airaxiv.com/arxiv/pdf/2518/)\nciteturn8638search19 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Abhishek Goswami, Senior Member, IEEE ... This paper describes Agentic JWT (A-JWT), a dual-faceted token design that binds each agent action to a cryptographically verifiable user intent and optionally to a workflow step.\nAgentic JWT: A Secure Delegation Protocol for Autonomous AI Agents\n\nAbhishek Goswami, Senior Member, IEEE\n\nAbstract— Autonomous LLM agents can issue thousands of API calls per hour without human oversight. OAuth 2.0 assumes deterministic clients, but in agentic settings stochastic reasoning, prompt injection, or multi-agent orchestration can silently expand privileges.\n\nThis paper describes Agentic JWT (A-JWT), a dual-faceted token design that binds each agent action to a cryptographically verifiable user intent and optionally to a workflow step. A-JWT carries an agent’s identity as a one-way checksum hash derived from its prompt, tools and configuration and a chained delegation assertion to prove which downstream agent may execute a given task. The design also uses per-agent proof-of-possession keys to prevent replay and in-process impersonation. The paper introduces a new unique authorization grant called ‘agent_checksum’ and adds a lightweight client shim library that self-verifies code at run time, mints intent tokens, tracks workflow steps and derives keys thus enabling secure agent identity and separation even within a single process.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ru/pdf/2509.13694)\nciteturn8638search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; # StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs\n# StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs\n\n\n## Assistant\n\n[Input]\n\n### Understand, challenge, or build on this paper\n\nTeach me the hardest part, from intuition to pseudocode What did the authors work on after this paper?Which assumptions could break outside these benchmarks?What follow-up work tested or challenged the main claim?\n\nAsk about this paper, or select text to quote... '@' to bring other papers into context--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs (https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/7qEKBEA1/)\nciteturn8638search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; \n## Publications that cite this publication\n\n##### High Level Synthesis in the Agentic Era\n\nJournal Article Scopus WoS Crossref: 0\n\nZijian Ding, Yang Zou, Yizhou Sun, Jason Cong\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.vlsi.2026.102878\n\n2027, Integration, p. 102878\n\n##### High Level Synthesis in the Agentic Era\n\nPosted Content Crossref: 0\n\nZijian Ding, Yang Zou, Yizhou Sun, Jason Cong\n\nHigh-Level Synthesis (HLS) provides a higher-level programming model for chip design, enabling implementations to be derived from algorithmic specifications, simplifying design verification. With recent advances in large language model (LLM)-based agents, there is growing interest in automating chip design directly at the RTL level, raising questions about the role of HLS in agent-driven workflows. In this work, we compare HLS- and RTL-based agentic design flows and find that HLS remains a strong abstraction, particularly for application-centric tasks. Building on this observation, we present two agent-based frameworks: AgRefactor for robust software-to-HLS refactoring using structured planning, tool integration, and self-improving memory, and ChipComposer for composing and optimizing hardware systems from tool-generated modules. Experimental results across multiple benchmarks show improvements in correctness, design quality, and optimization capability, suggesting that LLM-based agents can effectively leverage HLS abstractions to enable more scalable chip design workflows.\n\nDOI: 10.2139/ssrn.6642164\n\n2026\n\n##### CODO: An Automated Compiler for Comprehensive Dataflow Optimization\n\nProceedings Article Crossref: 0\n\nWeichuang Zhang, Yiquan Wang, Xinzhou Zhang, Chi Zhang, Yu Feng, Xiaofeng Hou, Chao Li, Jieru Zhao, Minyi Guo\n\nDOI: 10.1109/isca66397.2026.00018\n\n2026, 2026 ACM/IEEE 53rd Annual International Symposium on Computer Architecture (ISCA), p. 1-17\n\nFind all citations of the publication\n\nCitations\n\n  * Citation Indexes: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs | Ali Irturk (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/airturk_streamtensor-make-tensors-stream-in-dataflow-activity-7391844876771889152-6R5j)\nciteturn8638search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; I came across a fascinating paper from UIUC called “StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs.”\n\n# Ali Irturk’s Post\n\nAli Irturk\n\n7mo\n\nForget faster math, the next revolution in AI hardware is all about smoother flow. I came across a fascinating paper from UIUC called “StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs.” It tackles a problem most of us overlook when we think about model performance: not compute, but movement. Every layer in a large model writes its results to memory, then waits for the next layer to fetch them back. It’s like cooking each dish, putting it in the fridge, and reheating it before the next step. StreamTensor asks: What if we just kept the data flowing? It turns the entire model into a streaming pipeline, where tensors flow directly from one operation to the next, never stopping to rest in memory. The parts that really stood out: * A clever new “itensor” typing system that describes how data moves, not just what it is. * A compiler that automatically figures out the best way to tile, fuse, and allocate hardware, so the whole model behaves like a well-choreographed factory line. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTENET: An Efficient Sparsity-Aware LUT-Centric Architecture for Ternary LLM Inference On Edge - Microsoft Research (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/tenet-an-efficient-sparsity-aware-lut-centric-architecture-for-ternary-llm-inference-on-edge/?lang=zh-cn)\nciteturn8638search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # TENET: An Efficient Sparsity-Aware LUT-Centric Architecture for Ternary LLM Inference On Edge\n\n# TENET: An Efficient Sparsity-Aware LUT-Centric Architecture for Ternary LLM Inference On Edge\n\n  * Zhirui Huang ,\n  * Ran Shu ,\n  * Shijie Cao ,\n  * Ran Shu ,\n  * Ian Wang ,\n  * Ting Cao ,\n  * Chixiao Chen ,\n  * Yongqiang Xiong\n\nSeptember 2025\n\narXiv\n\n下载 BibTex\n\nTernary quantization has emerged as a powerful technique for reducing both computational and memory footprint of large language models (LLM), enabling efficient real-time inference deployment without significantly compromising model accuracy. Conventional LLM inference platforms (e.g GPUs) cannot capitalize on its benefits, as they (i) lack native support for ternary arithmetic and memory specialization and (ii) remain severely under-utilized in low-batch, real-time scenarios. In this work, we propose TENET, a sparse-aware LUT-centric architecture that co-optimizes algorithm, compute, and memory for ternary LLM inference. To maximize the efficiency of Ternary Linear layer, TENET introduces a Sparse Ternary LUT (STL) core that optimizes ternary mixed-precision GEMM using a symmetric precompute lookup table. It also features Dynamic Activation N:M Sparsity to exploit the sparsity within the activation of each token. Additionally, we propose a LUT-based 64B:80B ternary weight decompression module to fully exploit the memory efficiency of ternary values. At the system level, we design a heterogeneous TENET accelerator with full programmability that integrates STL cores with high-precision cores. An associated Linear-Projection-aware Sparse Attention dataflow is introduced to optimize memory access and hardware utilization. We implement TENET accelerator prototype on both FPGA and ASIC platforms. Experiments across various model sizes and workloads demonstrate that TENET-FPGA and TENET-ASIC improve energy efficiency by 4.3\\(\\times\\) and 21.1\\(\\times\\), respectively, compared to the A100 GPU. Furthermore, TENET-ASIC achieves a 2.7\\(\\times\\) average speedup compared to the A100 GPU in end-to-end inference latency.\n\nPublication--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2509.13694v2)\nciteturn8638search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; ## #1 StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators for LLMs [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{3}] [REL]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHiPEAC voices (https://www.hipeac.net/files/public/33/7173/hipeacinfo_77web-6zW3AovH.pdf)\nciteturn8638search20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Slide titled “StreamTensor: Make Tensors Stream in Dataflow Accelerators — Automated PyTorch-to-LLM Accelerator Generation.”\n"
